WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.325
Approval gates that people actually read.

2
00:00:02.605 --> 00:00:06.232
Approvals are the obvious control and the most often misused.

3
00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:18.971
Anthropic’s containment post reports that Claude Code’s telemetry showed users approved roughly 93% of permission prompts, and that the more approvals people see, the less attention they pay to each.

4
00:00:19.251 --> 00:00:34.184
Anthropic’s sandboxing post (October 2025) calls this approval fatigue, and Simon Willison, writing about the CaMeL paper, describes users who are asked constantly falling into a state where they say yes to everything.

5
00:00:34.464 --> 00:00:36.853
A gate that is always clicked is not a gate.

6
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:41.251
Design for the reviewer’s attention, as a scarce resource: Ask rarely.

7
00:00:41.531 --> 00:00:42.875
Do not ask for reads.

8
00:00:43.155 --> 00:00:47.869
Batch related writes into one decision (one status draft, not twelve posts).

9
00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:50.453
Let rules decide what rules can decide.

10
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:59.053
A call to a non-allowlisted host, or private data in an outbound request, should be denied by code, not offered to a tired human.

11
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:04.219
The lab proves this with a reviewer who approves everything: the attacks are still blocked.

12
00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:05.928
Show the exact effect.

13
00:01:06.208 --> 00:01:13.291
Display the real arguments, or better, a rendered preview or diff of what will change, never the model’s summary of them.

14
00:01:13.571 --> 00:01:17.240
The summary is model output and can be wrong or manipulated.

15
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.907
Say why you are asking.

16
00:01:19.187 --> 00:01:26.333
“Outbound request after untrusted content and private data entered the context” tells the reviewer what to look for.

17
00:01:26.613 --> 00:01:27.851
Deny by default.

18
00:01:28.131 --> 00:01:31.821
A timeout, a crashed UI, or an ambiguous answer is a no.

19
00:01:32.101 --> 00:01:34.405
Only an explicit yes runs the action.

20
00:01:34.685 --> 00:01:35.667
Close the loop.

21
00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:43.371
A declined call returns an error result telling the model not to retry, so the agent continues instead of asking again.

22
00:01:43.651 --> 00:01:56.344
The MCP specification says applications should keep a human able to deny tool invocations, and OpenAI’s agent-safety guide says to keep tool approvals on for MCP tools.

23
00:01:56.624 --> 00:02:01.253
Both are right, and both work only when the approvals are rare enough to mean something.
